    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

     <insight>
        <title>Key Takeaway</title>
        <p>
            The unit circle is defined as a circle with a radius of <m>1</m> unit, with its center at the origin of a coordinate plane.
        </p>
        <p>
            Any point <m>(x,y)</m> on the unit circle satisfies <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Because the radius is 1, the horizontal coordinate of the point is <m>\cos(\theta)=x</m> and the vertical coordinate is <m>\sin(\theta)=y</m>.
        </p>
           <p>
             Angles are measured from the positive <m>x -</m> axis. It is also important to note that positive angles are measured in the counterclockwise(left-hand side) direction, 
             while negative angles are measured in the clockwise(right-hand side) direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Kindly ensure that whenever you want to determine the quadrant in which an angle lies,begin from the positive <m>x -</m> axis and move(rotate) in the correct direction.
                </p>
            </hint>
            A better illustration of this is shown in the image below:
          </p>
          <p>
            <image source="path (no extension)">
                <shortdescription></shortdescription>
            </image>
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, if we pick the first quadrant, we can form a right-angled triangle with the angle <m>\theta</m> at the origin, the adjacent side along the positive <m>x -</m> axis, and the opposite side along the positive <m>y -</m> axis. The hypotenuse of this triangle is 1 (the radius of the unit circle), as shown in the image below.
          </p>
            <p>
                <image source="path (no extension)">
                    <shortdescription></shortdescription>
                </image>
            </p>
          <p>
              With reference to the image above, the coordinates are determined as follows:
              <ol>
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          The adjacent side is the horizontal distance from the origin to the point on the circle, which is <m>x</m>. Since the hypotenuse is 1, we have <m>\cos(\theta)= \frac{x}{1}= x</m>.
                      </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          The opposite side is the vertical distance from the origin to the point on the circle, which is <m>y</m>. Since the hypotenuse is 1, we have <m>\sin(\theta)= \frac{y}{1}= y</m>.
                      </p>
                  </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The tangent of the angle is the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side, which is <m>\tan(\theta)= \frac{y}{x}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
              </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            With respect to the Pythagorean identity,these ratios can be connected as follows:
          </p>
          <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta)=1</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>1+\tan^2(\theta)=\sec^2(\theta)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>1+\cot^2(\theta)=\csc^2(\theta)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
    </insight> 

